== Definition and Physical Meaning ==

From [[ψ Constitutive Relations]] (B1), the ψ permeability <math>\mu_\psi</math> relates the ψ flux density to the ψ current:

:<math>B_\psi = \mu_\psi\,\mathbf{J}_\psi</math>

where <math>\mathbf{J}_\psi = -(\partial_t\psi)\nabla\psi</math> is the ψ current density (= ψ Poynting vector) from [[ψ Conservation Laws]] (A2).

Physical meaning: <math>\mu_\psi</math> quantifies how much ψ flux flows per unit driving ψ current. High <math>\mu_\psi</math> — material is strongly permeable to ψ, flux concentrates easily. Low <math>\mu_\psi</math> — material resists ψ flux, acts as a ψ reluctor.

=== The Critical Engineering Connection ===

Unlike ψ permittivity (which primarily controls wave speed and impedance), ψ permeability has a second, more consequential effect: it directly shifts the effective mass of ψ inside the material.

From the modified wave equation in B1:

:<math>m_{\rm eff,mat}^2 = \varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi\,m_{\rm eff,vac}^2</math>

Therefore:

:<math>L_\psi(\text{in material}) = \frac{1}{m_{\rm eff,mat}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}\,m_{\rm eff,vac}} = \frac{L_\psi(\text{vacuum})}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Coherence length in material
| equation       = <math>L_\psi(\text{mat}) = \frac{L_\psi(\text{vac})}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}}</math>
| in_english     =
* High <math>\mu_\psi</math>: coherence length contracts — ψ field range shrinks. At fixed <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math>, doubling <math>\mu_\psi</math> halves <math>L_\psi</math>.
* Low <math>\mu_\psi \approx \mu_0</math>: coherence length approaches vacuum value — material is ψ-magnetically transparent.
* This is the engineering principle of the [[Psi Defender]]: by saturating the ferrofluid channels (maximum <math>\mu_\psi</math>), the shell contracts <math>L_\psi</math> to ~2 cm, creating a tight Yukawa screen around the operator.
* And the [[Psi Harmonizer]]: by demagnetising the ferrofluid (minimum <math>\mu_\psi \approx \mu_0</math>), <math>L_\psi</math> extends beyond 1 m for long-range group coupling.
}}

== The Ferrofluid Tuning System ==

=== Langevin Magnetisation Model ===

The ferrofluid (EMG-900, magnetite nanoparticles in water carrier) has a magnetisation that follows the Langevin function for non-interacting superparamagnetic particles:

:<math>M(H) = M_{\rm sat}\left[\coth\!\left(\frac{\mu_0 m_p H}{k_{\rm B}T}\right) - \frac{k_{\rm B}T}{\mu_0 m_p H}\right] \equiv M_{\rm sat}\,\mathcal{L}(\xi)</math>

where <math>\xi = \mu_0 m_p H / k_{\rm B}T</math> is the Langevin parameter and <math>\mathcal{L}(\xi) = \coth\xi - 1/\xi</math>.

'''EMG-900 parameters at 25°C:'''

{| class="wikitable" style="width:100%;"
! Parameter !! Symbol !! Value !! Source
|-
| Saturation magnetisation || <math>M_{\rm sat}</math> || 22.3 kA/m || Ferrotec datasheet
|-
| Mean particle diameter || <math>d_p</math> || 10 nm || TEM (B8 characterisation)
|-
| Single-particle moment || <math>m_p = M_{\rm sat,bulk}\cdot(\pi/6)d_p^3</math> || <math>4.8\times10^{-19}</math> A·m² || Calculated from magnetite bulk Msat = 480 kA/m
|-
| Thermal energy || <math>k_{\rm B}T</math> || <math>4.1\times10^{-21}</math> J || At 25°C
|-
| Langevin parameter at 1 kA/m || <math>\xi = \mu_0 m_p H/k_{\rm B}T</math> || 0.185 || Calculated
|-
| Initial susceptibility || <math>\chi_0 = \mu_0 M_{\rm sat}^2/(3k_{\rm B}T n_p)</math> || ~3.5 || Estimated from Msat and particle density
|}

=== μ_eff(H) from Magnetisation ===

The effective permeability of the ferrofluid at applied H-field:

:<math>\mu_{\rm eff}(H) = \mu_0\left(1 + \frac{M(H)}{H}\right) = \mu_0\left(1 + \frac{M_{\rm sat}}{H}\,\mathcal{L}\!\left(\frac{\mu_0 m_p H}{k_{\rm B}T}\right)\right)</math>

