== The μ_ψ–m_eff–L_ψ Engineering Chain ==

The full derivation chain from ferrofluid H-field set-point to [[Psi Stabilizer]] coherence length:

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = From H-field to coherence length — the four-step engineering chain
| step1_label = Set the H-field
| step1_text  = The [[Anchor Controller]] (D6) outputs a current <math>I</math> to the ferrofluid channel control coil. From solenoid geometry: <math>H = NI/l</math>. For the [[Psi Stabilizer]] Stabilizer target: <math>I \approx 0.02</math> A giving <math>H \approx 200</math> A/m.
| step2_label = Compute M(H) via Langevin
| step2_text  = <math>M(H) = M_{\rm sat}\,\mathcal{L}(\mu_0 m_p H/k_{\rm B}T)</math>. At H = 200 A/m: <math>\xi = 0.037</math>, <math>\mathcal{L}(\xi) \approx \xi/3 = 0.012</math>, <math>M \approx 0.012 \times 22.3 \approx 0.27</math> kA/m.
| step3_label = Derive μ_eff(H)
| step3_text  = <math>\mu_{\rm eff}(H) = \mu_0(1 + M/H) = \mu_0(1 + 270/200) = \mu_0 \times 2.35</math>. The channel permeability at this set-point is 2.35 times μ₀. The composite volume-averaged μ_ψ is much closer to μ₀ — weighted by ferrofluid channel volume fraction (~4%).
| step4_label = Compute m_eff in material
| step4_text  = <math>m_{\rm eff,mat} = \sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}\,m_{\rm eff,vac}</math>. With <math>\varepsilon_\psi \approx 4\varepsilon_0</math> and <math>\mu_\psi \approx 1.04\mu_0</math> (composite average): <math>m_{\rm eff,mat} \approx \sqrt{4 \times 1.04}\,m_{\rm eff,vac} \approx 2.04\,m_{\rm eff,vac}</math>.
| step5_label = Read off L_ψ
| step5_text  = <math>L_\psi = 1/m_{\rm eff,mat}</math>. If <math>m_{\rm eff,vac}</math> is calibrated so that <math>L_\psi^{\rm vac} = 40</math> cm (from TML-1 null measurement), then <math>L_\psi^{\rm (device)} = 40/2.04 \approx 20</math> cm — the Stabilizer design target.
| step6_label = Close the loop
| step6_text  = The [[Anchor Controller]] monitors RMSSD (proxy for operator <math>m_{\rm eff}^{\rm (bio)}</math> from A3 nonlinear shift) and adjusts H-field set-point to maintain <math>L_\psi^{\rm (device)} = L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math>. This is the Mk2.0 real-time coherence length matching function.
}}

