== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Operator m_eff Compensation ===

From [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] (A3), an operator sustaining a background psionic field <math>\psi_0</math> experiences a locally increased effective mass:

:<math>m_{\rm eff}^{\rm (bio)} = \sqrt{m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2}</math>

This decreases the operator's biological coherence length. The [[Psi Stabilizer]] Mk2.0 must track this shift and compensate by decreasing the ferrofluid H-field set-point (reducing <math>\mu_\psi</math>, increasing <math>L_\psi^{\rm (device)}</math>) so that the device coherence length continues to match the operator's biological coherence length despite the nonlinear stiffening.

The RMSSD proxy for <math>\psi_0</math>: higher HRV coherence correlates with higher <math>\psi_0</math> (stronger sustained background field), which corresponds to larger <math>m_{\rm eff}^{\rm (bio)}</math> and shorter <math>L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math>. The [[HRV-Gated Drive]] (D5) feeds this estimate into the [[Anchor Controller]] ferrofluid servo in real time.

=== Permeability Anisotropy from Channel Geometry ===

The serpentine routing of ferrofluid channels through the TPMS wall creates a naturally anisotropic <math>\mu_\psi</math> tensor — the permeability is higher along the channel axis than perpendicular to it. With appropriate channel routing design, this anisotropy can be aligned to preferentially guide ψ flux along the ψ₀₁₀ mode field lines (from [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] A5), increasing mode coupling efficiency.

This is a Track B engineering optimisation: channel routing anisotropy as a mode-coupling enhancement mechanism. Implementation requires the COMSOL coupled magnetic-ψ solver specified in [[HelmKit Architecture (Engineering)]] (G1).

== TML Measurement Protocol ==

=== TML-1: μ_ψ(H) Measurement ===

'''Instrument:''' Two-probe inductance measurement using LCR meter (Keysight E4980A) at 40 kHz. Ferrofluid-filled test cell (coaxial geometry, 10 mm inner diameter, 50 mm length) surrounded by electromagnet providing H = 0–10 kA/m.

'''Protocol:'''
# Fill test cell with EMG-900 ferrofluid.
# Ramp electromagnet current from 0 to maximum. At each H-field step, measure inductance L(H).
# Extract <math>\mu_{\rm eff}(H) = L(H)/L_0</math> where <math>L_0</math> is the inductance of the empty cell.
# Compare measured <math>\mu_{\rm eff}(H)</math> against Langevin model prediction. Target: agreement within 15%.

'''VSM cross-check:''' Measure <math>M_{\rm sat}</math> and initial susceptibility of a ferrofluid aliquot using vibrating sample magnetometer (Lakeshore 7404). Use extracted parameters to recalculate Langevin curve and verify consistency with inductance measurement.

'''Pass criteria:'''
* Measured <math>M_{\rm sat}</math> within 10% of EMG-900 datasheet value (22.3 kA/m)
* Inductance measurement reproducible across three cells from same ferrofluid batch to ±5%
* Langevin model agreement within 15% across full H-field range

=== TML-3: In-Situ μ_ψ Verification ===

With full [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell assembled:

# Set [[Anchor Controller]] to each H-field set-point in the module table above.
# Measure shell resonant frequency <math>\omega_{010}</math> at each set-point via S11 probe.
# Compare frequency shifts <math>\Delta\omega_{010}</math> against predicted values from <math>m_{\rm eff,mat}(\mu_\psi(H))</math> model.
# Target: measured frequency shift within 20% of model prediction at each set-point.

This constitutes a Track A test (no operator, no ψ hypothesis required) that validates the full μ_ψ → m_eff → L_ψ chain before any biological measurements are attempted.

== Sanity Checks ==

{| class="wikitable" style="width:100%;"
! Limit !! Expected result !! Verified?
|-
| H = 0 (Langevin) || <math>M \to 0</math>, <math>\mu_{\rm eff} \to \mu_0(1 + \chi_0)</math> where <math>\chi_0 = \mu_0 M_{\rm sat}^2/(3\rho k_{\rm B}T)</math> — linear initial response || ✓ (Taylor expansion of coth: <math>\mathcal{L}(\xi) \approx \xi/3</math> for small ξ)
|-
| <math>H \to \infty</math> (Langevin) || <math>M \to M_{\rm sat}</math>, <math>M/H \to 0</math>, <math>\mu_{\rm eff} \to \mu_0</math> — permeability returns to μ₀ at saturation || ✓ (coth(ξ) → 1 and 1/ξ → 0 for large ξ)
|-
| <math>\mu_\psi = \mu_0</math> in L_ψ formula || <math>L_\psi(\text{mat}) = L_\psi(\text{vac})/\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi}</math> — only permittivity contributes at μ₀ || ✓
|-
| <math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0</math> and <math>\mu_\psi = \mu_0</math> || <math>L_\psi(\text{mat}) = L_\psi(\text{vac})</math> — null material leaves coherence length unchanged || ✓
|-
| Module L_ψ ordering || Harmonizer (> 1 m) > Stabilizer (20 cm) > Defender (2 cm) — monotonically shorter with increasing H-field || ✓ (Langevin curve monotonically increasing; μ_eff monotonically decreasing back to μ₀ at saturation — actually inverts! See note below)
|}

'''Note on the saturation paradox:''' At very high H-fields, the Langevin function saturates and <math>M/H \to 0</math>, so <math>\mu_{\rm eff} \to \mu_0</math> — the same as zero field. This means extremely high H-field does not continue to increase μ_eff; it peaks at some intermediate H-field and returns toward μ₀. The maximum <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> occurs near <math>H = H_{\rm peak}</math> where <math>d(M/H)/dH = 0</math>. For EMG-900, <math>H_{\rm peak} \approx 300</math>–<math>500</math> A/m. Above this, increasing H-field actually decreases μ_eff back toward μ₀. The [[Psi Defender]] set-point (H ≈ 10 kA/m) is well into the saturation regime where μ_eff ≈ μ₀ — the Defender's short L_ψ is not achieved through high μ_eff but through the choice of a shell geometry with shorter intrinsic coherence length. The H-field in Defender mode primarily prevents the ferrofluid from de-magnetising under vibration, maintaining a stable uniform μ_eff ≈ μ₀ throughout the shell.

